Some things to consider when understanding the objective is that this lab is to simulate and observe how a client device communicates with a web server over a network. Another thing to consider would be this lab will be tracking the step-by-step flow of network traffic when a user requests a web page, highlighting how DNS resolves a domain name to IP address and how HTTP requests and delivers the web content.
First at the start of the lab I switched from Realtime Mode to Simulation Mode using the icon in the bottom-right corner of the workspace. Next in the event list Filter panel, I clicked Show All/None to clear all active protocols. Next I clicked Edit Filters, then selected DNS under the IPv4 tab, and selected HTTP under the Misc tab. I then Opended the PC interface, navigated to the Desktop tab, and launced the Web Browser. I then proceeded to enter the target web address www.example.com and clicked Go to intiate the request. I used the Capture/Forward button in the Simulation Panel to watch the packets travel between the PC and the server. I next clicked on individual event boxes in the Event List to inspect the PDU Information across the OSI model layers.
Some challenges I encountered was understanding the packet order because initially, it can be unclear why the web page does not load immediately. Stepping through the simulation frame-by-frame revealed that the PC cannont send an HTTP request until the DNS query successfully returns the server's IP address.
Some things I learned is that Using traffic protocol filters is essential for efficient network troubleshooting. I also learned that a browser relies on DNS to translate a human-readable URL into an IP address before HTTP can establish a connection and retrieve web data.
